<h2>BbScannerOptions</h2>
<pre><code>BbBarcodeFormat Formats                    = BbBarcodeFormat.All
BbScanRegion    Region                     = BbScanRegion.Full
int           MaxScanLines               = 15      // lines per orientation
int           DownsampleFactor           = 1
bool          TryHarder                  = false   // exhaustive detection, full rotation sweep
bool          AllowInverted              = false   // light on dark symbols
bool          TryReversedRows            = true    // linear symbols read right to left
bool          TryVerticalLines           = true    // columns and tilted lines, for rotated symbols
IReadOnlyCollection&lt;int&gt; ItfLengths      = 6..20 even
bool          Code39CheckDigit           = false   // require the modulo 43 check character
bool          Code39ExtendedMode         = false   // interpret Code 39 as full ASCII
TimeSpan      DuplicateSuppressionWindow = 2 seconds

BbScannerOptions.ForStillImages()   // 60 scan lines, TryHarder, AllowInverted, no suppression
options.Clone()                   // take a copy before editing a running scanner's options
options.Validate()                // throws on an out of range value</code></pre>

<p class="field__hint">
    ITF has no check character, so <code>ItfLengths</code> is the main defence against a
    truncated symbol being reported as a shorter, structurally valid number. Narrow it to the
    lengths your application actually uses.
</p>

<h2>Headless use</h2>
<p>
    The decoding pipeline has no dependency on Blazor or on a browser. It runs anywhere .NET
    runs, which is what makes it testable and benchmarkable.
</p>
<pre><code>using var decoder = new BbBarcodeDecoder(new BbScannerOptions
{
    Formats = BbBarcodeFormat.QrCode | BbBarcodeFormat.DataMatrix,
    DuplicateSuppressionWindow = TimeSpan.Zero,
});

var result = decoder.Decode(rgbaPixels, width, height, BbPixelFormat.Rgba32);
if (result is not null)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"{result.Format}: {result.Text}");
    Console.WriteLine($"took {decoder.LastFrame.Total.TotalMilliseconds:0.00} ms");
}</code></pre>

<h2>Extending the decoder</h2>
<p>
    A new symbology implements <code>IBbRowDecoder</code> for a linear code or
    <code>IBbMatrixDecoder</code> for a two dimensional one. Both receive already binarised input,
    so a new reader only has to contribute its own character table and structure.
</p>
<pre><code>public interface IBbRowDecoder
{
    BbBarcodeFormat Formats { get; }
    BbSymbolDecodeResult? DecodeRow(int rowNumber, BbBitRow row, BbBarcodeFormat enabledFormats);
}

public interface IBbMatrixDecoder
{
    BbBarcodeFormat Formats { get; }
    BbSymbolDecodeResult? Decode(BbBitMatrix image, BbMatrixDecodeOptions options);
}</code></pre>
<p>
    Binarisation is an extension point too: <code>IBbBinarizer</code> has one implementation tuned
    for matrix codes and one for linear rows, and a replacement can be substituted for either.
</p>
